  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The number of paramagnetic species from the following is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msub><mi>NiCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Cu</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mtext>&#160;and&#160;</mtext><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_a0aa2ce619d3.png\" style=\"width: 133px; height: 50px;\" /></p><p>It is diamagnetic as&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi>CN</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mo>-</mo></mmultiscripts></math>&nbsp;is a&nbsp;strong field ligand and forces electron pairing.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>:</mo><mi>Ni</mi><mo>=</mo></math><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_9e3973d938e3.png\" style=\"width: 123px; height: 50px;\" /></p><p>It is diamagnetic as carbonyl group is&nbsp;a&nbsp;strong field ligand and forces electron pairing.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msub><mi>NiCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math>&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_34b3beaef158.png\" style=\"width: 134px; height: 50px;\" /></p><p>It is paramagnetic as chloride&nbsp;group is&nbsp;a weak&nbsp;field ligand.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo></math>-&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_961e322152c3.png\" style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px;\" /></p><p>It is diamagnetic as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi>CN</mi></math>&nbsp;is a&nbsp;&nbsp;strong field ligand which causes pairing of electrons.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Cu</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;Here Copper ion has&nbsp;one unpaired electron, thus it is&nbsp;paramagnetic as it is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;configuration.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo></math>&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_31e090ac275a.png\" style=\"width: 129px; height: 50px;\" /></p><p>It is paramagnetic, here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>CN</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;is a&nbsp;strong field ligand</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo></math><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0148_4f13da2abadd.png\" style=\"width: 151px; height: 60px;\" /></p><p>It is paramagnetic, here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>&nbsp;, here a weak field ligand</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
